import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro'; const sections=[
{title:'Children and students',paragraphs:['School stress, anxiety, attention concerns, developmental differences, sleep changes, behaviour, and low mood can affect learning and family life. Assessment includes context and caregiver observations. A difficult week or one school incident does not by itself establish a diagnosis. Look for changes over time and how the child is functioning at home, school and with peers.','Write down specific examples: what happened before a difficult moment, what the child did or communicated, what support was offered and how it helped. Include strengths, preferred activities and situations that feel comfortable. Children may communicate distress through changes in sleep, attendance, appetite, behaviour, participation or physical complaints. These observations can help a professional understand what question to explore.']},
{title:'Practical support and age-based pathways',paragraphs:['Next steps may include parent guidance, developmental or psychological assessment, counselling, psychiatric review, school coordination or referral. All child-related care at Mind Grace is coordinated through <a href="/aasha">Aasha Child Development &amp; Early Intervention Centre</a>. Use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha appointment requests</a> for child and adolescent enquiries. Adult care uses the separate <a href="/book">Mind Grace booking route</a>.','The relevant starting point depends on the child’s age, concern and family priorities. A developmental assessment may help clarify communication, learning or attention questions. A therapy service may be more appropriate when a specific participation goal is clear. A psychiatric consultation can be discussed when medical or emotional-health questions need review. The clinic should explain who will be involved and what the appointment can answer before a family books.']},
{title:'Working with home and school',paragraphs:['Caregivers and educators may notice different parts of a child’s day. If the family agrees, observations from school can help clarify whether a concern appears across settings or in a particular routine. Ask what information should be shared, who will receive it and what consent is needed. A short example of a difficult transition or learning task may be more useful than a long list of labels.','Support plans should be practical for the child and the adults involved. A goal might focus on asking for help, making a transition more predictable, getting through a school task with suitable accommodations or participating in a preferred activity. Review whether a strategy improves comfort and participation, and tell the team if it increases distress or is hard to sustain.']},
{title:'Assessment, privacy and urgent situations',paragraphs:['Assessment scope, appointment count and report arrangements vary by question. Families can ask what the evaluation is intended to answer, who will conduct it, whether there will be written feedback and what the fee covers. Bring previous records or school feedback if available, but do not delay an enquiry while gathering documents. See <a href="/adhd-autism-assessment">ADHD and autism assessment information</a>, <a href="/learning-disability-assessment">learning assessment</a> and <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">Aasha developmental assessment</a>.','A child-development check-in can help a family organise observations but is not a diagnostic or validated screening test. Share only information needed for routine coordination through a booking form. If a child is in immediate danger or needs urgent medical attention, call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department; routine clinic channels are not emergency services.']}
];
